sequences/</link><pubDate>Fri, 03 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>admin</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/oeis-sequences/</guid><description>Fifteen integer sequences now published in the OEIS: nine authored, six extended. Computed by SAT solving and exhaustive search, checked against prior art, spread across seven fields.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/oeis-sequences/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Combinatorics</media:title></media:content><category>OEIS</category><category>combinatorics</category><category>Ramsey theory</category><category>extremal graph theory</category><category>additive combinatorics</category><category>SAT solving</category><category>integer sequences</category></item><item><title>The Expensive Half of a SAT Proof</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-05-symmetry-breaking-unsat/</link><pubDate>Fri, 05 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-05-symmetry-breaking-unsat/</guid><description>A from-first-principles tour of double-lex symmetry breaking for SAT: why the UNSAT side is expensive, how canonical-representative constraints fix it, a worked Zarankiewicz example that settled three new OEIS values, and an honest map of where the technique stops scaling.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-05-symmetry-breaking-unsat/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Combinatorics</media:title></media:content><category>sat-solving</category><category>symmetry-breaking</category><category>zarankiewicz</category><category>oeis</category><category>constraint-programming</category><category>extremal-graph-theory</category><category>unsat</category><category>lexicographic-ordering</category></item></channel></rss>